    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2044346">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2044346">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2044346)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2044346">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1401754804 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 Jun 2014 00:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2044346">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Joseph Keynes, director of the UIU Special Review council, flopped the thin blue file on his folding table.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I've noticed this in tales of late. Personally, I think that there has to be a better way of introducing characters in a more natural way that fits the flow of the story, rather than this sort of rote &quot;name rank serial number&quot; thing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A semi-circle of solemnly (and cheaply) dressed, yet expressionless UIU officials sat on worn chairs around the trembling representative standing before them.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't get this part. The panel is composed of UIU personnel? Then why are they so dubious as to what the mission of the division is? I'm also not entirely sure if &quot;solemnly dressed&quot; really makes a lot of sense here. Like, they're dressed in black funeral attire or something? Is it different from the normal business attire you'd find in an FBI office?</p>
<p>As a side note to this, not that you'd necessarily be expected to know this, but the organizational culture of the FBI is such that nice clothing is extremely important. Even in a marginalized unit, generally speaking agents aren't going to be wearing cheap suits, especially at the executive level.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Adjusting his tie and pushing his glasses up his sweating nose, UIU agent and legal specialist Damion Hill</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See my above remarks about introducing characters.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Silence.&quot; The Director folded his hands on the snap-up table, a gesture far more significant than what had just been conveyed.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't get what this sentence is trying to convey. Why is the guy folding his hands &quot;more significant than what had just been conveyed&quot;? What is the significance? This is an example, also, of several areas in the story where the writing is focusing more on telling the reader directly what is happening, rather than showing the situation through the actions and reactions of the characters.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He cocked his head at a handsomely dressed official carrying a grey briefcase quietly seated at the end of the semicircle.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I thought they were all dressed cheaply. Also, &quot;handsomely&quot; is an extremely subjective term, to the point that it doesn't convey any real specific meaning to the reader.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Relaxing back in his hard-backed chair, Keynes viciously added, &quot;I suggest you give him-and us-a good reason to refrain from proceeding as such.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Viciously&quot;? How so? Does he bark the the phrase at his subordinate? Is it a cold, slow remark full of contempt? Again, kind of a subjective term that doesn't really convey much as I imagine the story you're telling.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Why could you possibly need hundreds of agents to contain a few… Bigfeet, is that how you say it?&quot; A number of other agents laughed. Settling back into a cold look, she added, &quot;Mr. Hill, our organization's very goal is to remove any…&quot; she turned her nose at the term. &quot;Abnormal objects from society, without society noticing.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;what? Why does this person simultaneously mock someone from her own division for doing their job and then tell him what they're here to do?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>An all-black suited representative at the middle of the left column jeered, &quot;Who, the freaky Men In Black? Give me a break.&quot; Snickers were heard. Some began outright laughing. Keynes kept quiet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this makes no sense whatsoever. The guy himself is the Men in Black. He's even literally wearing a black suit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>&quot;Ladies and Gentlemen of the council, we are at war. This isn't some Vietnam, some guerrilla hiding in a tree that we can take out with a simple pistol shot, or a terrorist that we can arrest as soon as they go through a metal detector. We aren't even the ones fighting it. We can't come close to fighting it. These guerrillas burn us with their minds. They deflect our bullets with a swipe of a hand. These terrorists rip apart our metal detectors and use them to beat us to death.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Where to start. Firstly, using the Vietnam War as the &quot;easy and simple&quot; part of your analogy here seems extremely ill-considered. It's also kind of odd since these are civilian personnel. The over-the-top &quot;they beat us to death with our own tools and are bulletproof&quot; stuff just sounds too silly to take seriously, and makes this into a narm-filled harangue that sounds like action-movie dialogue more than anything else.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He paused for effect. The Resources representative looked shocked.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would they be shocked? <em>They lead the UIU</em>. Surely they are at least somewhat familiar with what they themselves do, right?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Adrenaline rushing from his eloquent start</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have the same issue with the term &quot;eloquent&quot; here that I do with the other vague descriptors I cited above.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>the handsomely dressed individual</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ditto.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>About five minutes later, the council filed back in. Keynes stood, while the rest sat, and the man with the briefcase silently exited the room. &quot;You have your years' funding, Mr. Hill,&quot; he sighed. &quot;This council is dismissed.&quot;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's not how funding in government agencies works at all. To make an extremely long story short, councils of executives do not make <em>any</em> decisions after talking for five minutes. That's especially true for decisions that involve what is likely to be tens of millions of dollars, at least. And it's almost certain that it's not the division's own call as to whether they'll get their funding, or even the FBI itself. I don't expect writers to be familiar with the workings of finance committees or public budgeting, but this part just completely beggars belief.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>TL;DR: I have issues with the writing itself, as well as what I feel to be a fairly inaccurate portrayal of bureaucracy, and a lack of internal logic to the piece.</p>
<p>Ultimately, I feel this to be yet another step on the road to the flanderization of the UIU, to the point of absurdity. Exec-level staff are not going to hold budget hearings on card tables. There's also a sort of disconnect in the mood of the article. I mean, they're talking about multiple deaths of federal agents. That would likely be a Congressional hearing in real life, but here it's sort of swept aside in favor of &quot;haw-haw&quot; style derision and Bigfoot jokes.</p>
<p>I'm not a fan of GoIs, but I wouldn't mind seeing whoever gets the UIU in the upcoming contest depart from the &quot;underfunded and stupid&quot; stereotype. I'm not sure how that originated or why it's so integral to the portrayal of a US government agency dedicated to anomalous phenomena. But I do find that trope, in this piece at least, to be sort of odd, compared to the sense of gravity that you seem to be have been going for, at least in part, for this article.</p>

							</div>

								
					
